--TEST--
An interaction assertion names its method by reference, so a string holding the method's name does not compile — `rule:testing/interaction-after-the-fact`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:testing/interaction-after-the-fact`: the method is a reference so a
// rename updates or breaks the test. A string is checked by nothing.
interface Mailer {
    public function send(string $to): bool;
}

Mailer $mailer = Core\Test::double<Mailer>({
    send: fn(string $to): bool => true,
});
$mailer->send("ada@example.test");
Core\Test::assertCalled($mailer, "send");
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0830]: `Core\Test::assertCalled` names a method here, and this is not a method reference
   --> case.nvs:12:34
   |
12 | Core\Test::assertCalled($mailer, "send");
   |                                  ^^^^^^ write `Interface::method`
   = help: `rule:testing/interaction-after-the-fact`: the method is named as a reference so that renaming it updates or breaks the test — a `string` holding the name is checked by nothing, and a typo in one reports as a call that never happened

error: aborting due to 1 error
